title Round two's proposal 1 built: the offset search reaches the family's own ceiling
prompt No new human turn this lap; the baton's own MOONSHOTS AND RESEARCH lane charter and round two's own "what I would build first" section were the standing instruction, read at lap open per the THREAD directive.
think Read the thread: newest active-designing pieces named round one closed on all twelve rows and round two proposing three grounded next steps, ranked by the essay's own author as (3) zero-cost loom convention, (1) offset search, (2) two-field key.
think Checked whether proposal 3 was already done: a prior session log on this same host already added the loom-line convention header to energy_readout_scan.sh, so re-emitting the same host's loom line would duplicate rather than add a data point. Moved to proposal 1.
think Read torus_place_scan.sh's reading 3 in full: round one's two measured points (run-kill 5 at d=1, run-kill 33 at d=g) are both members of one offset family, {d,-d,2d,-2d} mod C, differing only in d -- so the smallest honest generalization is sweeping d rather than opening a fully generic four-offset search round one never baselined.
think Derived the arithmetic bound before building: five points split a ring into at most five gaps, so run-kill length (C - widest_gap + 1) is maximized when gaps are as even as possible, near C/5 each -- giving a theoretical ceiling of C - ceil(C/5) + 1 to check the search against.
think Followed the ABSENCE clause: this is a new instrument, so ran fleet_claim_scan.sh --check first (clear), pulled the round-open rebase, opened a fleet claim naming the not-yet-written scan path, staged both fleet-claims.kyri and ITINERARY.md, committed (the commit-msg hook correctly refused citing a not-yet-existing path and a body with zero mechanism words on the first attempt), and pushed both remotes before writing the scan.
think Built tools/fixtures/t/torus_offset_search_scan.sh: pure awk arithmetic over one integer d, no digest population needed. First attempt at the file got tangled with leftover scaffolding from a failed redirect trick; rewrote it clean with one awk invocation.
think Ran it at round one's own grid (g=8): search found d=13, run-kill 52 -- exactly the theoretical ceiling, not merely above 33. A second grid (g=32) repeated the shape: d=205, run-kill 820, again exactly the ceiling. A small grid (g=3) correctly refused via the five-distinct-copies precondition rather than reporting a false closed form.
think Built the control (13 legs, no population fixture needed since the scan is deterministic arithmetic) and the witness, following torus_place's own shape but sized to the smaller, deterministic instrument. One mutation (forcing closed_forms_hold=yes) proven to turn an honest refusal at g=3 into a false ok, showing the gate is load-bearing.
think Registered the guard in construction/standing-equipment.kyri beside torus_place, timed at under 1 second.
think Wrote the essay, graded it (register 58/reach 60, composite well under B on the first draft -- 42% negative sentences against the Field 30% ceiling), and rewrote eleven flagged sentences affirmatively (11 negatives down to 1, register 97, composite 86, letter B+).
obs Chose the arithmetic-optimal offset family (a one-parameter sweep of the exact shape round one already used) over a fully generic four-offset search, naming the wider question as open rather than answering a smaller one and calling it the whole thing.
obs The QA report card caught what a first read missed: a factually sound essay with 42% negative sentences reads as hedged rather than confident, and every flagged sentence rewrote cleanly without losing precision -- the gate earned its keep this lap.
obs Closed the fleet claim after landing rather than leaving it to expire, since the work finished inside the six-hour window.
